title: LSP Hover

Rest the cursor on a filter, an operator, a macro call, a widget or a pragma line, and the editor shows what it amounts to in the running wiki, through `textDocument/hover`. Try it in [[LSP Hover - (Example)]].

!! What a filter hover shows

Put the cursor in a filter and the server runs it against the live wiki, reporting how many tiddlers match and naming up to 50 of them.

It runs in the context of the position you are hovering, not in a vacuum. `[all[current]]` means the tiddler the document is, taken from its own `title:` field so an unsaved rename counts, and an enclosing `<$let>` or `<$set>` overrides it exactly as the wiki would. The wiki's global macros are imported too, so a filter calling one resolves.

Filters are located through ~TiddlyWiki's own parser, so this covers a filtered transclusion (`{{{ ... }}}`), a `{{{ ... }}}` value of any attribute, any widget attribute named `filter`, `subfilter` or `$filter` in any quoting style, a filter spanning several lines, an `<%if%>` or `<%elseif%>` condition, and a `\function` body, which is a filter where a `\procedure` body is wikitext.

A backtick value is filled in first, `$(variable)$` and `${ filter }$` alike, as the widget would at that position, and the hover shows the filter that actually runs beside the one as written.

A line that is nothing but a filter is recognised too, so a filter in a documentation code block, or a filter-valued system tiddler such as `$:/config/FileSystemPaths`, can be hovered. Nothing there declares it a filter, so it has to parse to be reported: an unfinished or unparseable line stays silent rather than raising a filter error over ordinary prose.

A filter inside a definition body runs where the definition is written, so the definition's own parameters are empty there, and the hover says so rather than let an empty result pass for the filter's answer.

A filter under a cursor is usually still being typed, so four outcomes are kept apart and never share a message:

|! Outcome |! What you see |
|Unbalanced brackets |''Unfinished filter'', and it is not run |
|~TiddlyWiki rejects it |''Filter error'' with its own message |
|Runs, matches nothing |''Matches nothing'' |
|Nothing typed yet |''Empty filter'' |

The distinction matters because an unfinished filter and one that genuinely matches nothing look identical otherwise, and a confident wrong answer sends you looking in the wrong place.

!! What an operator or run prefix hover shows

Resting on an operator's name inside a filter explains the operator first, then the whole filter's result below a rule:

|! Written |! What you see |
|an operator, such as `compare:number:gt` |the module defining it, its suffix, `!` when negated, a link to its page on tiddlywiki.com for a core operator, and the description in its module's header |
|a shorthand prefix `+`, `-`, `~`, `=` or `=>` |the named prefix it stands for, `:and`, `:except`, `:else`, `:all` or `:let`, and what that does |
|a named prefix such as `:else` or `:cascade` |what it does and the module defining it; an unknown one is called out |
|a name no operator has, such as `toc-link[no]` |''Not a filter operator'': ~TiddlyWiki silently tests the field `toc-link` instead, as `[field:toc-link[no]]`, which is exactly what a misspelt operator looks like |
|a dotted name such as `my.fn[x]` |the function it calls, where one is visible; otherwise the same field test |

With a suffix, the field tested is the suffix, as ~TiddlyWiki does it. An operand keeps its own hover: a `<variable>` is a call.

The description is the text after the fields in the header of the module that runs, for a plugin's operator too. A header describes its whole module, so where one module exports several operators, as `$:/core/modules/filters/math.js` does, the hover says the header is shared.

!! What a widget hover shows

Resting on `<$name` reports the widget, the module defining it, and every attribute. An attribute is not always a literal: an indirect reference (`{{X!!field}}`), a macro (`<<var>>`) and a filtered value (`{{{ ... }}}`) are shown both as written and as what they come to here, which is the part that cannot be read off the page.

A `<$let>` or `<$vars>` also reports the variables it binds, and a `<$set>` the variable it names rather than its attribute names.

A widget nobody registers is called out. ~TiddlyWiki renders an unknown widget as nothing at all, so a misspelt name is otherwise completely silent.

A custom widget from a `\widget` definition is reported as one, with where it is defined, because it takes over the tag before any built-in widget does.

Last comes what it actually renders, body and all, taken from the render already done for the position rather than a second one. A filter attribute is ''not'' run here: point at the filter itself for that, and the two hovers stay distinct rather than repeating each other.

!! What an `<%if%>` hover shows

An `<%if%>` block is a `$list` underneath, but it is hovered as what was written. The `<%if%>` keyword lists every clause and which one renders at that position, and each `<%if%>` or `<%elseif%>` condition is hovered as a filter that also says whether it holds.

!! What a macro call hover shows

Resting on `<<name ...>>` reports what it is, where it is defined, and what every parameter is worth at that call: the argument given by name, the one given by position, or the declared default you never wrote. An argument the definition does not declare is listed as well, because a misspelled parameter name is exactly the mistake worth seeing.

Positional arguments bind the way the wiki binds them: a `\procedure` or `\widget` gives parameter N the Nth positional argument, while a `\define` or `\function` gives each parameter not named in the call the next positional argument left over. One that no parameter takes is listed as ''ignored'', because ~TiddlyWiki drops it without a word.

A parameter holding a filter is then run, so `<<list-links [tag[LSP]]>>` also reports the tiddlers it will list.

The call comes from the parse tree and the definition from a second parse of the tiddler declaring it, which is where the parameter names and defaults come from. The definition is found the way the wiki finds it: one nested in the body around the call first, then one in the tiddler being edited, then one an `\import` or `<$importvariables>` around the call brings in, then a global the wiki imports for every tiddler, from a tiddler tagged `$:/tags/Macro` or `$:/tags/Global`, and last a ~JavaScript macro.

A call inside another definition's body is hovered too. ~TiddlyWiki keeps a `\procedure`, `\define` or `\widget` body as plain text, so the server parses it separately.

A `<$transclude $variable="name">` or `<$macrocall $name="name">` is a widget and a call at once, so its hover describes the widget first and then the call it makes.

A name that is no definition is still explained: a parameter of the enclosing definition with its default, a variable set by an enclosing widget with the line it is set on, or a variable ~TiddlyWiki sets itself, such as `currentTiddler`. The innermost binding wins, as it does when the wiki renders, so a parameter named `tag` is that parameter even though the core also defines a `tag` macro. A name nothing binds is reported as ''not set here'': only a caller can give it a value.

A `<<var>>` written as an attribute value is hovered as a call of its own rather than as the widget around it.

!! What a pragma line hover shows

A line starting a definition or another pragma explains itself:

* a keyword such as `\procedure`, `\end`, `\import` or `\whitespace` says what it does
* a definition keyword written after other content is flagged: ~TiddlyWiki reads a pragma only where nothing but pragmas precedes it, so there it is plain text
* a definition's name gives its kind, its reach (global, local to this tiddler, or local to the definition around it), its parameters with their defaults, any global of the same name it hides, and how often the name is called: here, in other files, and in tiddlers without a file
* a parameter gives its default and how often its own body names it; a count of 0 does not mean unused, since a definition the body calls can read it too

!! Opening what you hover

Every matched title in a hover is a link to its own `.tid` file. A shadow tiddler has no file of its own, so in a hover list it stays plain text. A definition in a shadow tiddler or a tiddler without a file links its read-only view in the editor, and names the plugin supplying it; the core is never named. The definition of a core macro is also linked into the browser, whenever the same process is serving HTTP.
